{"name":"Flowering Cactus","hex":"#a2d4bd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flowering","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of flower"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The period of time during which a plant is in flower.","The period of time during which someone or something is at its peak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flowering"},{"term":"cactus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the family Cactaceae, a family of flowering New World succulent plants suited to a hot, semi-desert climate.","Any succulent plant with a thick fleshy stem bearing spines but no leaves, such as euphorbs."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Non-functional, broken, exhausted, dead."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cactus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower"},{"title":"Cactus","blocks":[{"text":"A cactus is a member of the plant family Cactaceae, a family of the order Caryophyllales comprising about 131 genera with some 1,866 known species. The word cactus derives, through Latin, from the Ancient Greek word κάκτος (káktos), a name originally used by Theophrastus for a spiny plant whose identity is now not certain. Cacti occur in a wide range of shapes and sizes. They are native to the Americas, ranging from Patagonia in the south to parts of western Canada in the north, with the exception of Rhipsalis baccifera, which is also found in Africa and Sri Lanka. Cacti are adapted to live in very dry environments, including the Atacama Desert, one of the driest places on Earth. Because of this, cacti show many adaptations to conserve water. For example, almost all cacti are succulents, meaning they have thickened, fleshy organs adapted to store water. Unlike many other succulents, the stem is the only part of most cacti where water storage takes place. Most species of cacti have lost true leaves, retaining only spines, which are highly modified leaves. As well as defending against herbivores, spines help prevent water loss by reducing air flow close to the cactus and providing some shade. In the absence of true leaves, cacti's enlarged stems carry out photosynthesis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Various_Cactaceae.jpg/330px-Various_Cactaceae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cactus"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Phalaenopsis_bellina_%28Blue_Pink_x_sib%29_%28Rchb.f.%29_Christenson-_Brittonia_47-_58_%281995%29._20210623_013709.jpg/500px-Phalaenopsis_bellina_%28Blue_Pink_x_sib%29_%28Rchb.f.%29_Christenson-_Brittonia_47-_58_%281995%29._20210623_013709.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Phalaenopsis_bellina_%28Blue_Pink_x_sib%29_%28Rchb.f.%29_Christenson-_Brittonia_47-_58_%281995%29._20210623_013709.jpg/1280px-Phalaenopsis_bellina_%28Blue_Pink_x_sib%29_%28Rchb.f.%29_Christenson-_Brittonia_47-_58_%281995%29._20210623_013709.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2